Fillets: two stripes on the same face and on one plane whose edges a
corner of the solid interrupts are joined by one blend over the corner,
cut by the corner's sides, which grow up to it (mitcad#343).

The case: a housing whose top is a plane, with a bulge (a tilted cylinder)
on its rounded vertical corner, the bulge's foot on the top rounded. The
foot is two edges, each from inside the top to the corner's rounding,
which it reaches just past its tangent lines. The ball of each rounding
rolls on between the top's plane and the bulge past that end: over the
corner its contact on the plane leaves the top across the first side's
top edge and comes back across the second side's, while its contact on
the bulge stays on the bulge. OCCT ended each stripe at the end of its
edge, and the corners there filled the gap wrongly (an invalid solid, or a
failed build).

- ChFi3d_Builder::JoinAcrossCorners (Compute, after JoinOverNarrowFaces):
  pairs of free stripe ends of one constant radius, at most 8 radii apart,
  whose last pieces lie on the same face Fo and on faces of one plane (or
  the same face), at vertices held both by a third face C (the corner).
- ChFi3d_Builder::JoinAcrossCorner: one walk from the middle of the first
  stripe's last piece along a guide through the ball centres of both last
  pieces, the plane unbounded and Fo bounded. Where its contact on the
  plane leaves the first face and comes onto the second (classified), the
  edges crossed give the sides; the edges between the sides and C, run on
  past their vertices, meet the blend's surface at the junctions. The
  blend is cut by each side and by C between those points (their surfaces'
  intersection, interpolated with its p-curves), and split into five
  pieces: on the first plane face, on the three cuts, and on the second
  face up to a section within the second stripe's last piece, where the
  two stripes share an edge. The points where the contact crosses the
  sides' edges keep each edge's part beyond the blend: their transition on
  the edge is set by the side of the point its vertex on C lies on (the
  walk may run either way along the spine, the edges' curves either way
  along it). The first stripe's pieces past the walk's start and the
  second's past the meeting are removed.
- The sides grow up to the cuts: the edges between them run on to new
  vertices at the junctions (curve interferences on both faces), and the
  edges of C with the plane faces and Fo, now under the blend, are dropped
  (vertex interferences TopAbs_EXTERNAL, as for the chamfer cut by the
  faces beyond it).

Upstream: new.
